    "evidence_text": "However, the converted singing voice can be easily out of key, showing that the existing approach can not model the pitch information precisely.\n\non.\n\nThere are several singing voice conversion methods to convert one's singing voice to another , , .\n\nGo back to step 1.\n\nAudio samples are available at our website .\n\nEvery singer provided 4 songs.\n\nDuring testing, We converted each one's singing voice to the other five singer's voice.\n\nBefore training, We converted the songs to monophonic audio of\n\n16kHz sample rate and PCM-16 bit format.\n\nDuring the training process, backtranslation\n\nand mixup  were employed to improve the conversion.\n\nThe automatic score roughly followed the design in .\n\nThe higher the score is, the better the output pitch matches the input pitch.\n\nPitchNet.\n\nTABREF14.\n\nOur method performed better both on conversion and reconstruction.\n\n(naturalness) (2) How well does the converted version match the original?\n\nAs shown by Tab.\n\nWe used different pitch as input to observe how the output pitch\n\nwould change along with the input pitch.\n\nAnd the output pitch was also extracted by the pitch tracker of the librosa package.\n\nConclusion\tIn this paper, a novel unsupervised singing voice conversion method named PitchNet is proposed.",
